e decimal-point alignment behavior.\n\n## Behavior Details\n\n### Left Mode\n- Numbers formatted with consistent decimal places\n- Left-justified within column\n- Right-padded with spaces\n- Null treated as literal \"null\", right-padded\n\n### Right Mode (Default)\n- Numbers formatted with consistent decimal places\n- Right-justified within column\n- Left-padded with spaces\n- Null treated as literal \"null\", left-padded\n- Maintains backward compatibility\n\n### Decimal Mode\n- Decimal points aligned vertically across rows\n- Computed based on maximum digits before/after decimal\n- Spaces inserted before/after as needed\n- Null left-padded to align with decimal position\n\n### Normalize Mode\n- All numbers have identical width\n- Fixed decimal places for all values\n- Guarantees rows have equal character length\n- Useful for fixed-width output requirements\n\n## Nesting Support\nThe alignment mode is applied consistently at all nesting depths because:\n1. The `NumberListAlignment` option is passed from `Formatter` to `TableTemplate`\n2. `TableTemplate` passes the alignment mode to all child templates\n3. Alignment is computed per-column at each level independently\n\n## Compatibility\n- Existing `DontJustifyNumbers` option still works\n- When `DontJustifyNumbers = true`, alignment is disabled\n- When `NumberListAlignment = Left`, numbers are not reformatted (treated as strings)\n- Default `NumberListAlignment = Right` maintains existing behavior\n- All other options (line length, EOL style, whitespace omission) are respected\n\n## Testing\nThe implementation includes comprehensive tests covering:\n- All four alignment modes\n- Null value handling\n- Large numbers\n- Negative numbers\n- Scientific notation (invalidates alignment as expected)\n- Nested arrays\n- Table formatting\n- Row length equality for Normalize mode\n",
